+	<!-- custom: similarly to how was done with the spy but with some differences too, we beautify the missonaries placeHistory in sevopedia unit, while removing unrelated info from the religion concept, or inaccurate one, in particular adding caveats about how base Civ4 info may have different rules than the mod running this. A bit tedious to read perhaps but hopefully clearer while helping to localize the relevant info, while assuming (a bit higher) user base level knowledge, still largely informational. -->
 	<TEXT>
 		<Tag>TXT_KEY_MISSIONARY_UNITS_GENERIC_DESCRIPTION_WITH_INFO_ON_ALL_CIVS_NO_CIV_PEDIA</Tag>
-		<English>[TAB][BOLD]Religion Spreading:[\BOLD][PARAGRAPH:1]Religions spread randomly from city to city. Religions tend to spread from a city possessing the religion to nearby cities as well as to cities the possessing city has trade routes with. Some religious buildings (see below) increase the speed at which a religion will spread, and a civilization's religious civic (see below) can have a great effect upon the spread of religions, as well. Missionaries allow you to control the spread of religion.[PARAGRAPH:2][BOLD]Missionary Units:[\BOLD][PARAGRAPH:1]Civilizations can construct special "missionary" units to spread the civilization's state religion. Each civilization may have only a limited number of missionary units in play at any one time, and each may cost two gold per turn to maintain or maybe another value depending on your mod's settings.[PARAGRAPH:2][BOLD]Building Missionaries[\BOLD][PARAGRAPH:1] In base Civ4, civilizations using the [LINK=CIVIC_ORGANIZED_RELIGION]Organized Religion[\LINK] civic can construct missionaries in any city with the state religion; and if a civilization is not following Organized Religion, it can build missionaries only in cities with Monasteries. Note that this may or not vary for your/in your mod's specific settings and is provided as general information only.[PARAGRAPH:2][BOLD]Moving Missionaries[\BOLD][PARAGRAPH:1]Once constructed, missionaries can move about the map like any other unit. Your missionaries can freely enter your territory or neutral territory; in base Civ4, they cannot enter another civilization's territory until you have an [LINK=CONCEPT_DIPLOMACY]Open Borders[\LINK] agreement with that civ, which may also vary or not depending on your mod's settings. [PARAGRAPH:2][BOLD]How Missionaries Spread Religion[\BOLD][PARAGRAPH:1]When the missionary is in the target city, click on the "spread religion" order in the missionary's unit box; if the attempt is successful the missionary's religion is now in the target city. In base civ4, religions spread more easily into other cities within the missionary's civilization, which may or not vary based/depending on your mod's specific settings as well. In base Civ4 also, further, it is easier to spread a religion into a city with few or no religions already present; each resident religion decreases the odds of success; these rules may or not vary in your mod based on the mod's specific settings as well. In base Civ4, there is no limit to how many religions may be present in a single city; all seven religions can coexist in the same city, which may also vary or not depending on your mod's settings, and is provided as general information as well, as well as for the next sentence. The missionary is consumed in the conversion attempt, successful or not.[PARAGRAPH:2][BOLD]History[\BOLD][PARAGRAPH:1]A lot of information is available about what missionaries are or do, for the main religions in/of the world, represented or not in base Civ4 or your mod. You can view this page for a lot more details if interested: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Missionary</English>
+		<English>[TAB][BOLD]Religion Spreading:[\BOLD][PARAGRAPH:1]Religions spread randomly from city to city. Religions tend to spread from a city possessing the religion to nearby cities as well as to cities the possessing city has trade routes with. Some religious buildings (see below) increase the speed at which a religion will spread, and a civilization's religious civic (see below) can have a great effect upon the spread of religions, as well. Missionaries allow you to control the spread of religion.[PARAGRAPH:2][BOLD]Missionary Units:[\BOLD][PARAGRAPH:1]Civilizations can construct special "missionary" units to spread the civilization's state religion. Each civilization may have only a limited number of missionary units in play at any one time, and each may cost two gold per turn to maintain or maybe another value depending on your mod's settings.[PARAGRAPH:2][BOLD]Building Missionaries[\BOLD][PARAGRAPH:1] In base Civ4, civilizations using the [LINK=CIVIC_ORGANIZED_RELIGION]Organized Religion[\LINK] civic can construct missionaries in any city with the state religion; and if a civilization is not following Organized Religion, it can build missionaries only in cities with Monasteries. Note that this may vary for your/in your mod's specific settings and is provided as general information only.[PARAGRAPH:2][BOLD]Moving Missionaries[\BOLD][PARAGRAPH:1]Once constructed, missionaries can move about the map like any other unit. Your missionaries can freely enter your territory or neutral territory; in base Civ4, they cannot enter another civilization's territory until you have an [LINK=CONCEPT_DIPLOMACY]Open Borders[\LINK] agreement with that civ, which may also vary depending on your mod's settings. [PARAGRAPH:2][BOLD]How Missionaries Spread Religion[\BOLD][PARAGRAPH:1]When the missionary is in the target city, click on the "spread religion" order in the missionary's unit box; if the attempt is successful the missionary's religion is now in the target city. In base civ4, religions spread more easily into other cities within the missionary's civilization, which may vary depending on your mod's specific settings as well. In base Civ4 also, further, it is easier to spread a religion into a city with few or no religions already present; each resident religion decreases the odds of success; these rules may vary in your mod based on the mod's specific settings as well. In base Civ4, there is no limit to how many religions may be present in a single city; all seven religions can coexist in the same city, which may also vary depending on your mod's settings, and is provided as general information as well, as well as for the next sentence. The missionary is consumed in the conversion attempt, successful or not.[PARAGRAPH:2][BOLD]History[\BOLD][PARAGRAPH:1]A lot of information is available about what missionaries are or do, for the main religions in/of the world, represented in base Civ4 or your mod. You can view this page for a lot more details if interested: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Missionary</English>
 	</TEXT>
 
 
