+	<!-- custom: Source: https://historiska.se/en/explore-history/history-hub/the-viking-aristocracy/ (GPT-5.6 Sol + GPT-5.6 Thinking) -->
 	<TEXT>
-		<Tag>TXT_KEY_BUILDING_SCANDINAVIA_TRADING_POST_PEDIA</Tag>
-		<English>Although they did seek to extend their empire through piracy and invasion, for much of the time the Vikings were merchants, with a trading network that extended from Greenland, Iceland, and Finland into the British Isles, Western Europe, and the Mediterranean. Viking merchants are known to have traded Russian slaves at Constantinople for silks and spices, and they brought furs and ivory from far-away Greenland to ports throughout Europe, North Africa and the Middle East.[NEWLINE][NEWLINE]The earliest Viking trading posts were quite primitive settlements, consisting of simple dwellings and warehouses constructed of whatever local material was readily available. Over time many of the posts flourished, becoming important cities in their own rights. For example the Irish capitol of Dublin began its existence as a Viking trading post; and after being captured by the Vikings, the English city of York became a thriving trading center, second in importance only to London.</English>
+		<Tag>TXT_KEY_BUILDING_SCANDINAVIA_CHIEFTAINS_HALL_PEDIA</Tag>
+		<English>[TAB]In Viking Age Scandinavia, political and military power was often centered on aristocratic estates. At the heart of such an estate stood a great hall, used for feasts, religious ceremonies, the reception of guests and envoys, councils, and the public display of the lord's status. Archaeological evidence shows that major hall complexes could be surrounded by dwellings, stables, workshops, and quarters associated with the lord's household and armed followers.[PARAGRAPH:1]Scandinavian rulers and magnates maintained retinues of loyal warriors whose service strengthened both their military power and their political authority. No single standardized institution called a "Chieftain's Hall" existed throughout Scandinavia; the term broadly describes the great hall and household of a local magnate, where hospitality, authority, communal gathering, and martial service were closely connected.</English>
+	</TEXT>
+
+
+
+	<!-- custom: Sources: https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/anatolian-studies/article/mesopotamian-gardens/0F1655D8252D5F0F09E43BE1D251CC93 ; https://www.britishmuseum.org/blog/paradise-earth-gardens-ashurbanipal (GPT-5.6 Thinking) -->
+	<TEXT>
+		<Tag>TXT_KEY_BUILDING_BABYLON_GARDEN_PEDIA</Tag>
+		<English>[TAB]Gardens and orchards were an established part of the cultivated landscape of ancient Mesopotamia, where dry conditions made controlled irrigation essential. Canals and smaller channels carried water to planted areas, allowing trees, vines, vegetables, and other crops to be grown near settlements, temples, and elite residences.[PARAGRAPH:1]Mesopotamian gardens could serve both practical and prestigious purposes. Fruit trees and vines produced valuable food and drink, while shaded planted spaces displayed the ability of households, temples, and rulers to organize water, labor, and fertile land in an arid environment. Their maintenance therefore depended on the same irrigation knowledge that supported the wider agricultural economy of Babylonia.[PARAGRAPH:1]Later classical authors made Babylon especially famous for the legendary Hanging Gardens, although their historical existence and precise location remain debated. More ordinary gardens, orchards, and irrigated plantations were nevertheless a well-established feature of Mesopotamian agriculture and urban life.</English>
 	</TEXT>
 
 
