+	<TEXT>
+		<Tag>TXT_KEY_BUILDING_GERMAN_ZUNFTHAUS_PEDIA</Tag>
+		<English>[TAB]A Zunfthaus is a house belonging to a Zunft, or guild. From the Middle Ages onward, craftsmen in German-speaking towns organized themselves into guilds that regulated their trades, defended professional interests, controlled entry into the craft, and sought to maintain standards of workmanship. Guild rules could shape apprenticeship, the path from journeyman to master, methods of work, and many aspects of a craftsman's economic and social life.[PARAGRAPH:1]Guild life also required places to meet. Wealthier guilds maintained their own guild houses, inns, or meeting rooms, which could serve as gathering places for members and travelling journeymen and could hold the guild's records, seals, money, and ceremonial possessions. The Zunfthaus thus became an institutional center of urban craft life, linking skilled production with training, standards, mutual organization, and communal affairs.</English>
