+		<English>[TAB]Furusiyya was a broad tradition of horsemanship and martial training in the medieval Islamic world. Its literature and practice covered the handling and care of horses, riding, the lance, archery, swordsmanship, simulated combat, and other exercises intended to prepare mounted warriors for war. Surviving Mamluk manuals show that specialist instructors taught trainee soldiers in royal barracks and preserved a substantial body of technical knowledge concerning cavalry, weapons, and horse management.[PARAGRAPH:1]A maydan was an open ground or square and could serve as a place for riding, mounted exercises, displays, and military training. In Mamluk Cairo and other major centers, cavalrymen practiced on horseback in such spaces after receiving more individual instruction. Stable facilities, trained horses, instructors, and open exercise grounds together provided the practical setting in which the skills associated with furusiyya could be developed and maintained.</English>
