+	<TEXT>
+		<Tag>TXT_KEY_BUILDING_CARTHAGE_PUNIC_AGORA_PEDIA</Tag>
+		<English>[TAB]In ancient Carthage, the agora formed one of the principal centers of public and commercial life. Ancient accounts place it between the city's harbors and the heights of Byrsa, in a position connecting maritime commerce with the political heart of the city. Although "agora" is a Greek term meaning a gathering or market place, it is conventionally used to describe this Carthaginian public district.[PARAGRAPH:1]The agora was more than a marketplace. Merchants and citizens could gather there for business and public affairs, while nearby spaces probably accommodated religious shrines, government activity, assemblies, and the administration of justice. This combination of commerce and civic life reflected the character of Carthage itself: a densely urbanized state whose political power was closely connected with the wealth generated by Mediterranean exchange.[PARAGRAPH:1]Carthage's harbors supported trade and cultural contacts across much of the Mediterranean, helping make the city one of the principal commercial centers of the western Mediterranean world. The original Punic buildings of the agora have not survived, and their precise appearance remains uncertain, but the district occupied an important place between the maritime, civic, and political spheres of the Carthaginian capital.</English>
