+		<English>[TAB]The Dutch word beurs refers to an exchange: a place where merchants, financiers, and later investors met to conduct business. Amsterdam had recognized exchange locations before the seventeenth century, first for merchants trading goods and bills. As Dutch overseas commerce expanded, these meeting places developed into increasingly organized financial markets.[PARAGRAPH:1]A major turning point came in 1602, when the Dutch East India Company, or VOC, issued shares to a broad investing public. Trading in those shares quickly developed in Amsterdam, while the city established the Bank of Amsterdam in 1609 and opened Hendrick de Keyser's dedicated exchange building in 1611. Amsterdam subsequently became one of the most important centers of early modern finance and securities trading.</English>
